Kirk and I have never used a full 3D direct product RKHS interpolation.  Even
his original version of the program did the RKHS in R and theta for fixed r,
then did a 1D RKHS interpolation on r.  We later changed the final 1D
interpolation to a cubic fit, as I described earlier. 

So, I have implemented your idea of drop-a-single-point on 2D grids only, and
done it for each different value of r in our ab initio set.    It would be
easy to extend the idea to the final 1D interpolation as well, though it's
not clear to me that you would learn much.

Here's how it works.  As Rabitz points out (JCP 114, 3940), if a particular
grid point has exactly the ab initio energy that you would get by dropping
that point and interpolating over all the others, then the RKHS coefficient
for that particular grid point will be 0.  So what I do is to calculate how
much the energy at a particular grid point would have to change by in order
to force its coefficient to zero.  That change is the difference
between the ab initio energy and the value that would be interpolated for it
if it was missing.

It's very easy to figure out what that change must be.  You get the RKHS
coefficients by solving the linear system

Q*c = V,

where Q is the matrix of kernel values at the grid points, Q_{ij} =
Q(x_i, x_j), c is the vector of RKHS coefficients, and V is the vector of
potential values at the grid points.  Formally the solution to this system
is

c = Q^{-1}V.

In practice we find Q^{-1} by singular value decomposition, but we still end
up with effectively an inverse matrix.

Now, I want to find a new vector V', all of whose elements are the same as
those of V except one, and I want that one to force the corresponding element
of c to zero.  I write c + dc = Q^{-1} (V + dV) where dc and dV both have
elements that are all zero except for a single one, at position k.  dc_k =
-c_k, and dV_k = delta, the estimated error I am looking for.  I have

c + dc = Q^{-1} V + Q^{-1} dV

dc = Q^{-1} dV

Considering only the kth element of dc, I have

dc_k = \sum_i (Q^{-1})_{ki} dV_i

and because all the elements of dV are zero except for that with i=k, I get

dc_k = (Q^{-1})_{kk} dV_k

or, finally,

dV_k = dc_k/(Q^{-1})_{kk}

       = -c_k/(Q^{-1})_{kk}

So, to estimate the error in a particular ab initio grid point energy, you
simply divide the RKHS coefficient for that point by the corresponding
diagonal element of the inverse kernel matrix.

I have carried out this program for Kirk's points, considering each R, theta
plane with a given value of r separately.  Attached is a file that gives, in
the same order as in kpdata.pot, the ab initio energy at the grid point,
the RKHS interpolated energy at that grid point, and the (negative of the)
estimated error obtained as = just described.  Let me know what you think.

